<!-- ADHD IN CHILDREN -->
<section id="adhd-children" class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
          <path d="M12 6v6l4 2"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">ADHD (Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder)</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">ADHD is one of the most common neurodevelopmental disorders in children, affecting approximately 5-7% of school-aged children in India. It's characterized by persistent patterns of inattention, hyperactivity, and impulsivity that interfere with functioning and development.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Three Types of ADHD</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-2-col gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Predominantly Inattentive</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Difficulty organizing tasks, following instructions, and staying focused. Often appears daydreamy or forgetful.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Predominantly Hyperactive-Impulsive</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Excessive fidgeting, talking, interrupting others, and difficulty waiting turns. Often appears restless.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Combined Presentation</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Significant symptoms of both inattention and hyperactivity-impulsivity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Common Signs in Children</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Difficulty paying attention to details or making careless mistakes</li>
        <li>Trouble sustaining attention during tasks or play</li>
        <li>Not seeming to listen when spoken to directly</li>
        <li>Failing to follow through on instructions or chores</li>
        <li>Difficulty organizing tasks and activities</li>
        <li>Avoiding tasks requiring sustained mental effort</li>
        <li>Losing things necessary for tasks (toys, homework, pencils)</li>
        <li>Easily distracted by external stimuli</li>
        <li>Forgetfulness in daily activities</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="notice bg-warm p-4 rounded mb-4">
        <strong>Important:</strong> ADHD is not caused by bad parenting or too much sugar. It's a legitimate neurodevelopmental disorder with strong genetic components.
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Effective Interventions</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Behavioral therapy and parent training</li>
        <li>School accommodations and support</li>
        <li>Medication (stimulants or non-stimulants) when appropriate</li>
        <li>Organizational skills training</li>
        <li>Regular exercise and structured routines</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/book/" class="btn btn-primary">Schedule ADHD Assessment</a>
        <a href="/conditions/" class="btn btn-outline">Learn About All Conditions</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ANXIETY IN YOUNG PEOPLE -->
<section id="anxiety-children" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <path d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">Anxiety Disorders in Children & Teens</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">Anxiety is the most common mental health concern in children and adolescents. While some anxiety is normal and even helpful, excessive anxiety can interfere with school, friendships, and family life.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Types of Childhood Anxiety</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-2-col gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Separation Anxiety</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Excessive fear of being away from parents or caregivers, common in younger children.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Social Anxiety</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Intense fear of social situations, being judged, or embarrassing themselves, common in teens.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Generalized Anxiety</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Excessive worry about multiple areas (school, sports, family, future).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Specific Phobias</h4>
          <p class="text-sm">Intense fear of specific objects or situations (animals, heights, doctors).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Physical Signs of Anxiety in Children</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Frequent stomachaches or headaches without medical cause</li>
        <li>Complaints of feeling sick before school or activities</li>
        <li>Difficulty sleeping or nightmares</li>
        <li>Clinginess or crying more than usual</li>
        <li>Restlessness or fidgeting</li>
        <li>Avoidance of certain situations or places</li>
        <li>Perfectionism or excessive worry about mistakes</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="bg-warm p-6 rounded-lg mb-4">
        <h3 class="h3 mb-3">How Parents Can Help</h3>
        <ul class="icon-list mb-4">
          <li><strong>Validate feelings:</strong> Acknowledge their fears without dismissing them</li>
          <li><strong>Teach coping skills:</strong> Deep breathing, positive self-talk, gradual exposure</li>
          <li><strong>Maintain routines:</strong> Predictability reduces anxiety</li>
          <li><strong>Model healthy coping:</strong> Show how you manage your own stress</li>
          <li><strong>Limit avoidance:</strong> Gently encourage facing fears in small steps</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/services/#child-adolescent-care" class="btn btn-primary">Get Help for Your Child</a>
        <a href="/butterfly-tapper/" class="btn btn-outline">Try Calming Tool</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DEPRESSION IN TEENAGERS -->
<section id="depression-teens" class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <path d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">Depression in Teenagers</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">Teenage depression is more than just moodiness or typical adolescent behavior. It's a serious mental health condition that affects how teens think, feel, and behave. Early recognition and treatment are crucial for recovery.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Warning Signs in Teenagers</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Persistent sadness or irritability</li>
        <li>Loss of interest in friends and activities they once enjoyed</li>
        <li>Changes in eating or sleeping patterns</li>
        <li>Declining grades or school refusal</li>
        <li>Feelings of worthlessness or guilt</li>
        <li>Difficulty concentrating</li>
        <li>Fatigue or lack of energy</li>
        <li>Thoughts of death or suicide</li>
        <li>Self-harm behaviors (cutting, burning)</li>
        <li>Substance use as a way to cope</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="notice bg-warning p-4 rounded mb-4">
        <strong>Critical:</strong> Teenage depression often looks different than adult depression. Teens may appear more irritable than sad and may act out rather than withdraw. Any talk of suicide should be taken seriously and requires immediate professional attention.
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Risk Factors</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-2-col gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Biological Factors</h4>
          <ul class="icon-list text-sm">
            <li>Family history of depression</li>
            <li>Chemical imbalances in brain</li>
            <li>Chronic illness</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Environmental Factors</h4>
          <ul class="icon-list text-sm">
            <li>Bullying or peer pressure</li>
            <li>Academic stress</li>
            <li>Family conflict or trauma</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/book/" class="btn btn-primary">Book Teen Assessment</a>
        <a href="/emergency/" class="btn btn-outline">Crisis Resources</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- BEHAVIORAL ISSUES -->
<section id="behavioral-issues" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">Behavioral Issues in Children</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">Behavioral problems in children can range from mild defiance to severe oppositional behavior. Understanding the underlying causes is key to effective intervention.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Common Behavioral Concerns</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li><strong>Oppositional Defiant Disorder (ODD):</strong> Pattern of angry, defiant behavior toward authority figures</li>
        <li><strong>Conduct Disorder:</strong> More severe pattern of violating rights of others and social norms</li>
        <li><strong>Disruptive Mood Dysregulation Disorder:</strong> Severe temper outbursts inconsistent with developmental level</li>
        <li><strong>Adjustment Disorders:</strong> Emotional or behavioral symptoms in response to a stressor</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Underlying Causes to Consider</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-2-col gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Medical Factors</h4>
          <ul class="icon-list text-sm">
            <li>Undiagnosed learning disabilities</li>
            <li>Hearing or vision problems</li>
            <li>Sleep disorders</li>
            <li>Nutritional deficiencies</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="surface-soft p-4 rounded">
          <h4 class="h4 mb-2">Emotional Factors</h4>
          <ul class="icon-list text-sm">
            <li>Anxiety or depression</li>
            <li>Trauma or abuse</li>
            <li>Grief or loss</li>
            <li>Family changes (divorce, moving)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Effective Strategies</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Parent-Child Interaction Therapy (PCIT)</li>
        <li>Consistent rules and consequences</li>
        <li>Positive reinforcement for good behavior</li>
        <li>Clear communication and active listening</li>
        <li>Professional assessment to identify root causes</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/book/" class="btn btn-primary">Schedule Behavioral Assessment</a>
        <a href="/process/" class="btn btn-outline">Our Approach</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- AUTISM & DEVELOPMENTAL CONCERNS -->
<section id="autism-development" class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <path d="M4 4a2 2 0 00-2 2v12a2 2 0 002 2h16a2 2 0 002-2V6a2 2 0 00-2-2H4z"/>
          <path d="M12 8v8m-4-4h8"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">Autism Spectrum & Developmental Concerns</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) is a developmental disability that affects how people communicate, interact, learn, and behave. Early diagnosis and intervention can significantly improve outcomes.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Early Signs of Autism (Ages 1-3)</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Limited or no eye contact</li>
        <li>Not responding to their name by 12 months</li>
        <li>No babbling or pointing by 12 months</li>
        <li>No single words by 16 months</li>
        <li>No two-word phrases by 24 months</li>
        <li>Loss of previously acquired language or social skills</li>
        <li>Repetitive movements (hand flapping, rocking)</li>
        <li>Unusual attachment to objects</li>
        <li>Difficulty with changes in routine</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Signs in Older Children</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Difficulty understanding social cues and body language</li>
        <li>Trouble making or keeping friends</li>
        <li>Intense focus on specific topics or interests</li>
        <li>Literal interpretation of language</li>
        <li>Sensitivity to sounds, textures, or lights</li>
        <li>Preference for routines and sameness</li>
        <li>Difficulty with transitions</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="notice bg-warm p-4 rounded mb-4">
        <strong>Hope:</strong> With early intervention, speech therapy, occupational therapy, and educational support, children with autism can develop important skills and lead fulfilling lives.
      </div>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/book/" class="btn btn-primary">Developmental Assessment</a>
        <a href="/doctors/" class="btn btn-outline">Meet Our Specialists</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WHEN PARENTS SHOULD WORRY -->
<section id="when-to-worry" class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <article class="surface p-6 rounded-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4">
        <svg class="icon icon-lg text-accent" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/>
          <path d="M12 8v4m0 4h.01"/>
        </svg>
        <h2 class="h2">When Should Parents Seek Professional Help?</h2>
      </div>
      
      <p class="lead mb-4">It's natural for parents to worry about their child's behavior and development. Here are clear indicators that professional evaluation may be beneficial.</p>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Red Flags Requiring Immediate Attention</h3>
      <div class="bg-warning p-6 rounded-lg mb-4">
        <ul class="check-list">
          <li>Any talk of suicide or self-harm</li>
          <li>Self-injurious behaviors (cutting, burning, hitting themselves)</li>
          <li>Severe aggression toward others or animals</li>
          <li>Complete withdrawal from family and friends</li>
          <li>Sudden dramatic changes in behavior or personality</li>
          <li>Inability to perform basic daily activities</li>
          <li>Hallucinations or delusions</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      
      <h3 class="h3 mt-6 mb-3">Concerns Warranting Professional Evaluation</h3>
      <ul class="check-list mb-4">
        <li>Symptoms lasting more than 2-4 weeks</li>
        <li>Problems interfering with school performance</li>
        <li>Difficulty maintaining friendships</li>
        <li>Frequent conflicts at home</li>
        <li>School refusal or frequent calls from school</li>
        <li>Regression to earlier developmental stages</li>
        <li>Extreme emotional reactions disproportionate to situations</li>
        <li>Parent intuition that something isn't right</li>
      </ul>
      
      <div class="bg-warm p-6 rounded-lg mb-4">
        <h3 class="h3 mb-3">What Happens During a Child Psychiatric Evaluation?</h3>
        <ol class="icon-list mb-4">
          <li><strong>Parent Interview:</strong> Discussion of concerns, developmental history, family history</li>
          <li><strong>Child Interview:</strong> Age-appropriate conversation with the child</li>
          <li><strong>Observation:</strong> Watching how the child interacts and behaves</li>
          <li><strong>Rating Scales:</strong> Standardized questionnaires for parents and teachers</li>
          <li><strong>School Input:</strong> May request teacher feedback or school records</li>
          <li><strong>Feedback Session:</strong> Review findings and recommend treatment plan</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      
      <div class="notice p-4 rounded mb-4">
        <strong>Remember:</strong> Seeking help early doesn't mean your child has a "serious problem." Many issues are easier to address when caught early, and some concerns may simply require guidance and support during a difficult transition.
      </div>
      
      <div class="cta-row mt-6">
        <a href="/book/" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Book Child Assessment</a>
        <a href="/faq/" class="btn btn-outline btn-lg">Frequently Asked Questions</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

